 ],
 "literal": "Desire, desire, they say, desire / is not torment and sickness indeed; having shrunk, / throbbing and subsiding are not indeed; like the deep musth / of an elephant having chewed green leaves, / it also possesses restraint, if indeed it obtains beholders.",
 "translation": "People speak of desire, desire, and desire. But desire is neither a tormenting spirit nor a disease. It does not shrink, nor does it have a throbbing rise and a subsiding fall. Like the deep musth of an elephant that has chewed green leaves, it also has a sense of restraint, if only it meets those who can perceive it.",
 "modern": "மக்கள் காமம் காமம் என்று சொல்கிறார்கள். காமம் என்பது வருத்தும் தெய்வமோ அல்லது நோயோ அல்ல. அது சுருங்கி, மிகுந்து தோன்றுவதும் பின்பு தணிந்து போவதும் இல்லை. தழைகளைத் தின்ற யானையின் மிகுந்த மதநீர் போல, அதனைக் காண்பவரைக் கண்டால் அது அடங்கித் தோன்றும் தன்மையையும் உடையது.",
 "explanation": "The hero speaks about the nature of his love for the heroine. People often speak of desire as if it were a disease or a tormenting spirit, but the hero explains that it is far more constant and deep. It does not fluctuate wildly, throbbing and subsiding, nor does it shrink away. He compares his love to the deep musth of an elephant that has fed on green leaves. Just as the elephant's musth can be restrained or delayed when it encounters a skilled handler or onlooker, his intense passion is kept under control and hidden out of restraint when he is in the presence of others who might observe him.",
